From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Oct 2026 08:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARMJIT_A64: PINREGS, guest registers kept in host registers
 across linked blocks (MELONDS_JIT_PINREGS=<hex mask>, default off)

A block loads the guest registers it uses at its entry and stores the ones it
wrote at its exit; with ~40k block exits a frame that is a big share of the
emulator thread. With MELONDS_JIT_PINREGS=<hex mask of r0-r14> (at most 7
registers; MELONDS_JIT_PINREGS7 for the ARM7, else the same mask) each masked
guest register has a fixed home in the callee-saved w19-w25 and stays there
across linked blocks:
- The register cache pins them (loaded from the block's start, never unloaded,
  never stored by an exit).
- The block's entry prologue (0007's FASTENTRY slot, EntrySkip) loads them: the
  loop's way in (ARM_Dispatch). Linked exits, DynExit (its base literal includes
  EntrySkip) and self loops enter past it with the registers live.
- Every way back to the loop goes through RetLoop = RetStub, a JIT stub that
  stores the CPU's pinned registers and goes on to ARM_Ret (ARM_Ret itself when
  the variable is unset).
- C that reads or writes R[0-14] has them stored before and loaded after: the
  interpreter fallbacks (Comp_PinnedSync round the call, the MCRNop far path),
  UpdateModeTrampoline and JumpToTrampoline (PushRegs/PopRegs' hi-register
  store and reload: a pinned one is stored, not unloaded). The slow memory
  paths, SlowBlockTransfer and SliceChain9 don't touch R[0-14] (callee-saved
  survive them). NocashPrint's register dump from a slow write reads memory:
  log text only.
- Reset re-reads the variables and refills RetStub, EntrySkip and DynExit's
  bases, so an in-run A/B can flip the mask at a block cache reset.
Exact: the same values reach every reader (memory copies are brought up to
date before any C that reads them and reloaded after any C that writes them);
no cycle changes.
